David Damerell <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> writes:
> [ Some kind of time limit on midgame to smack down the unrighteous
>   who play "patience games" like pudding farming? ]
>
> You're assuming that the clock should be both tight and a sharp
> cutoff.  Quite the opposite; I want a clock that is fairly liberal,
> and which gradually drains the player of resources in some way (like
> the post-Rodney effects, but much less drastic). This keeps the
> "what else" question, but adds "is that worth the time it will
> take?"

Oh, *that*. That's a good idea. So good, in fact, that we already have
it. Two times over, what's more.

First, my video gaming time is a scarce, precious, non-renewable
resource. I already have to ask myself "is P-farming / _-squatting /
extinctionism / whatever worth the time?" and I already find that I
must answer "no."  There doesn't need to be an in-game mechanism; the
needs of real life and stuff to do in the Big Blue Room accomplish
this nicely, thanks.

Second, within nethack itself, there are deadly monsters such as the
spotted brainfart and the arch-typo. You can't genocide them or even
see them coming, and they can always instakill you. The only partial
defenses are care and thinking before you press the key -- and boredom
degrades these defenses. Every turn I spend doing something
uninteresting, YASD slithers closer.

On a slightly less tongue-in-cheek note, I suppose that I'm
philosophically opposed to adding another turn limit, even a generous
one that bleeds you to death through a thousand cuts rather than one
swift stroke. (I'm not even all that thrilled about how Rodney
currently works, to be honest.)

For me personally, nethack is all about dying because I tried to
advance too quickly. If I die, let it be because I got greedy,
impatient or careless. Losing because I'd gotten to a point where
resources were decaying faster than I could earn them, and I still
didn't have enough to make a credible attempt at a run, doesn't strike
me as fun.

-D., killed by an imperious order. 

